;; A literal lambda applied to the wrong number of arguments.
;;
;; Reported by `Normalize`, where the rewrite fires, rather than left to
;; inference. At that node both counts are known and the callee is a lambda
;; written right there, so the mistake can be stated as one; inference sees two
;; arrow types that will not unify and can name neither the lambda nor the
;; count.
;;
;; This is also why the rewrite does not quietly decline on a mismatch. An
;; applied lambda with the wrong arity is never anything but an error, and
;; leaving the `EApp` alone would trade a precise message for a worse one.

;; EXPECT-ERROR: takes 2 argument(s) but is applied to 3

(: sum (-> int))
(defun (sum)
  ((fun (x y) (+ x y)) 1 2 3))

(defun (main args)
  (println (int->string (sum)))
  0)
